 ### [Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering](4614-volume-level-of-detail-lod-rendering.md)
-Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering is a method that maximizes image quality within user-specified frame rate and system constraints (RAM, texture memory, bandwidth) by adaptively modifying the set of rendered cells in an octree. It differs from sibling methods like [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md) by explicitly maintaining an "active cut" of octree nodes to ensure space is covered exactly once, and by integrating data loading from disk/RAM into the adaptation loop. It builds on [Resolution Adaptation](3063-resolution-adaptation.md) by using error metrics to drive the structural changes in the volume representation rather than just step size or viewport scaling.
+Volume Level-of-Detail (LOD) Rendering is a method that maximizes image quality within user-specified frame rate and system constraints (RAM, texture memory, bandwidth) by adaptively modifying the set of rendered cells. It differs from static LOD methods by maintaining an "active cut" of octree nodes that covers the model space exactly once, dynamically splitting or merging nodes to balance visual error against performance. Unlike sibling methods that may rely on global resolution factors or fixed hierarchies, this approach uses a queue-based error metric to drive local refinement or coarsening in real-time.
 
 ### [Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering](8163-focus-and-context-multiresolution-rendering.md)
-Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering is a technique to balance processing capacity between a high-detail focus region and a lower-detail context region to improve interactivity in time-critical applications. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md) by explicitly separating the scene into two distinct rendering passes with different resolutions and rendering models, rather than adjusting a single global resolution or using hierarchical data structures. The goal is not seamless integration but a tradeoff between quality and speed.
+Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering is a technique that balances processing capacity between a high-detail focus region and a lower-detail context region to improve interactivity in time-critical applications. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by explicitly decoupling the rendering parameters (resolution, lighting, sampling) of the context from the focus, rather than relying on a single global metric or hierarchical data structure. The goal is not seamless integration but a tradeoff between achievable quality and speed.
 
 ### [Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB)](8219-multiresolution-atlas-buffer-mab.md)
-The Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB) is an acceleration wrapper for Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) in Virtual Reality (VR) that reduces ray density adaptively based on peripheral vision and Head-Mounted Display (HMD) lens distortion, then reconstructs the full-resolution image via compositing. It differs from sibling methods like [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md) by operating as a post-process/compositing bracket around existing raycasters rather than modifying the volume data structure or traversal logic itself, and unlike [Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md) which adjusts voxel grid resolution, MAB maintains the original ray traversal but subsamples the output framebuffer.
+The Multiresolution Atlas Buffer (MAB) is an acceleration wrapper for Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) in Virtual Reality (VR) that reduces ray density adaptively based on peripheral vision and Head-Mounted Display (HMD) lens distortion, then reconstructs the full-resolution image via compositing. It differs from sibling methods like [Level-of-detail (LOD) methods](1007-level-of-detail-lod-methods.md) or [Dynamic Volume Resolution & Alpha Mask Update](2539-dynamic-volume-resolution-alpha-mask-update.md) by decoupling the raycasting resolution from the final framebuffer resolution through a bidirectional mapping system, rather than modifying the volume data structure or the ray traversal logic itself. Unlike [Focus and Context Multiresolution Rendering](8163-focus-and-context-multiresolution-rendering.md) which may rely on high-speed eye-tracking, MAB assumes a fixed gaze center relative to the HMD lens distortion, making it suitable for standard VR setups without specialized eye-tracking hardware.
 
 #### Mixed Resolution Scene Rendering
-Combines full-resolution scene elements with low-resolution high-density volume data by rendering the volume to off-screen targets and projecting them onto a quad [S1]. Setup involves two off-screen cameras (one per eye) and two low-resolution render targets (e.g., 200x200) [S1]. Each frame, the low-resolution targets are drawn for each eye (containing volume data and expensive shaders), the scene is drawn normally at full resolution (meshes, UI), and a quad is drawn in front of the user to project the low-res renders onto it [S1].
+- [S1] Combines full-resolution scene elements with low-resolution high-density volume data by rendering the volume to off-screen targets and projecting them onto a quad.
+- [S1] Setup two off-screen cameras, one to follow each eye that update each frame.
+- [S1] Setup two low-resolution render targets (that is, 200x200 each) that the cameras render into.
+- [S1] Set up a quad that moves in front of the user.
+- [S1] Each Frame: Draw the render targets for each eye at low-resolution (volume data, expensive shaders, and so on).
+- [S1] Each Frame: Draw the scene normally as full resolution (meshes, UI, and so on).
+- [S1] Each Frame: Draw a quad in front of the user, over the scene, and project the low-res renders onto that.
+- [S1] Result: visual combination of full-resolution elements with low-resolution but high-density volume data.
 
