 ## Background
-Resolution adaptation in volume rendering originated from the need to handle large datasets interactively. Early LOD methods for unstructured grids and voxel data focused on reducing polygon counts or slice counts [S2, unofficial][S3]. With the advent of GPU-based raycasting and neural volume representations (NeRFs), dynamic resolution became critical for managing training costs and inference latency [S1][S2, unofficial]. In Virtual Reality, where frame rates are critical to prevent cybersickness, dynamic viewport scaling and peripheral vision-based resolution adaptation (MAB) became standard practices [S1].
+Resolution adaptation in volume rendering has evolved from static level-of-detail techniques for interactive visualization [S1][S2, unofficial][S3] to dynamic runtime systems for VR [S1] and training-time optimizations for neural radiance fields [S1]. Early methods focused on mesh simplification and texture slicing [S2, unofficial], while modern implementations leverage hierarchical data structures like octrees [S1] or neural field representations with periodic upscaling [S1]. In VR, techniques like the Multiresolution Atlas Buffer address the specific constraints of HMD lens distortion and peripheral vision [S1]. For neural volumes, dynamic resolution is often coupled with alpha mask culling to manage memory during training [S1].
 
